=base:summary.cmark
The HTTP(S) endpoints that add A, AAAA and TXT records to a running microdns, and the shared-secret HMAC that authenticates them.

=base:main-content.cmark

# Dynamic registration

Records that are not in the [MicroZone file](/microzone-files.html) are
registered while microdns runs, by calling its HTTP(S) API. They are kept in
memory and lost on restart. There are only a few registrations available.

## Endpoints

Expose the client IP as `A` (or `AAAA` for IPv6 clients) for
`${subzone}.${apex}`, mostly for the dynamic-DNS problem. The client IP is
taken from the `x-forwarded-for` header if present:

- `POST /register/auto/:subzone?apex=:apex`

Expose `A` or `AAAA` records (the syntax follows
[`Net.IPv4.decode` and `Net.IPv6.decode`](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/ip-1.7.7/docs/Net-IPv4.html#v:decode)):

- `POST /register/a/:subzone/:value?apex=:apex`
- `POST /register/aaaa/:subzone/:value?apex=:apex`

Expose `TXT ${subzone}.${apex} ${value}`, mostly for ACME-challenge tokens:

- `POST /register/txt/:subzone/:value?apex=:apex`

## Authentication

An HMAC-256 with a shared secret provides a minimum of authentication (see
`MicroDNS.DynamicRegistration.verifyHmac`). Requests carry the header:

```
x-microdns-hmac: ${base16-encode(hmac(${secret_key},${apex}${subzone}))}
```

In its present form, the implementation lacks nonces and only hashes the
apex and subzone. Queries that can be spied on can therefore be modified and
replayed. As a result, if you use the dynamic-registration API over the
Internet, TLS should be mandatory.

Note that the apex is the empty string when missing from the URL query
parameters: it is not replaced with the one given on the command line. Said
another way, the secret key is the only command-line parameter that
influences the HMAC.

